window.docContents[215].resolve({"/Encapsulation/Objects/#Core-C______--Encapsulation--Objects":{"contents":"The expression new C(args) allocates one location per field of the whole chain of , the root base first, and then the tagged record. It runs the constructors from the root base down, each with this bound to the object. The constructor of  receives the arguments and the others none, since there are no initialiser lists.A class without a constructor is created by new C() and keeps the default values.\n\nThe call of a member body, a method, a constructor or a destructor, binds this to the location  of the receiver as an alias. It binds the parameters as the call of a function does, by value with a fresh copy and by reference with an alias. The return frees the copies and the locals of the body, never the receiver.The predicate  of  gives the result. A non void member that ends without return has no derivation, an error of the evaluator.\n\nA method call e.m(args) needs a receiver of class type and e->m(args) a receiver of pointer type. The method is the nearest  in the chain of the static class of the receiver, it must be visible, and the arguments are checked as in a call. An unqualified call m(args) inside a member body is this->m(args). A method that returns a reference has the rules of , which read the location it returns.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Encapsulation","header":"6.2. Objects","id":"/Encapsulation/Objects/#Core-C______--Encapsulation--Objects"}});